course: dictionary
lesson: 1130
title: One word, many meanings: breath
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the context tells you which one is meant. Breath is a good example. Breath is the air that goes into and out of your lungs, but «hold your breath» under water means stopping your breathing for a while, and «a breath of fresh air» means something new and pleasant.
explain: Look at the words near the word. «Out of breath» comes after running or hard work, so it means breathing fast. «Under your breath» means very quietly, because the words around it are said softly. Swap your meaning into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably got it right.
explain: Breath also shows feelings and calm. «Take a deep breath» helps you slow down when you are cross or nervous. A breath of wind is different again: it is a light movement of air, and nobody is breathing at all.
fact: Breath is the air that goes into and out of your lungs.
fact: A breath is one time of breathing in and out, so «one breath» means one count of breathing.
fact: «Out of breath» means breathing fast because you ran or worked hard.
fact: «Catch your breath» means rest until your breathing slows down.
fact: Breath is uncountable when it means the air itself, so we say «breath», not «breaths».
exercise: «Blowing too hard makes a recorder jump up to a shrill squeal; a soft, steady breath gives a clear note.» In this sentence, what is a breath? | A whole day of rest | *One steady puff of air blown out | A loud shout across the room | A shrill squeal from the recorder
exercise: «He rattled off the list all in one breath.» What does all in one breath mean? | In a loud, angry voice | With a long, slow sigh | Very quietly, under his breath | *Without stopping to take a breath
exercise: Mia says «take a deep breath» means run very fast. What does it really suggest? | To blow out all the candles | *To breathe in slowly and deeply to calm yourself | To hold something under water | To run very fast to the gate
test: «She spoke under her breath so the teacher could not hear.» What does under her breath mean? | Slowly, one word at a time | *Very quietly | Under the water | Loudly and angrily
test: Which sentence uses breath to mean a light movement of air? | «Hold your breath under the water.» | «I needed a breath after that steep climb.» | *«We could feel a breath of wind on our faces.» | «She took a breath before she began to sing.»
exam: In «Scientists use drones to collect whale breath from the spray», what does breath mean? | Something new and pleasant | A light wind on a summer day | Holding your breath under water | *The air a whale breathes out
